From: cactus@bb.hks.net (L. Todd Masco)
Date: 31 Oct 1994 02:19:11 -0500
Subject: [#94-10-609] Argument by Analogy (Uploads)

I find most of this discussion of computer-environment intelligence
to be somewhat off-putting in its assumptions.  I, for one, doubt that
there is a seperable aspect to humans called consciousness: our thoughts
and feelings are the result of a machine evolved to consider complex
situations and evaluate them for their relative pay-offs from previous
experience and imagined possible scenarios.  The "I" I think about is
merely the point of view for which I have the strongest confidence
because of direct experience.

A common (not universal, but still pervasive) assumption in this 
discussion seems to be that intelligence in a computer environment will
be intuitively recognizable (in this case, by analogy) by others as
similar to the human "self."

I'd be very surprised if this turned out to be true.

A great deal of human interaction is based upon our biological
predispositions built into our genes by millions upon millions of years
of evolution in a hostile environment.  Our entire method of evaluating
ideas, for example, is frequently based first upon an evaluation of the
necessity of a fight-or-flight reaction because we evolved in an
environment where that's the highest priority task for survival.  We
protect our ideologies as if they were territory, because our distant
ancestors had to defend their territory in order to procreate.
We frequently react to disagreement as threats to our identity, since
we largely store our identity in our ideals.

I expect AIs, however they manifest, to be as much shaped by their
environment in thought-forms as we are by ours.  This makes it very
difficult to make arguments by analogy when analogy is based upon
very superficial behaviors.

I expect this to be true of uploads, as well.  If I don't need to spend
my first few cycles determining whether or not the phenomenon I'm
considering is going to leap for my throat, I'm going to edit those
cycles out.  When I'm uploaded, I'll be much closer to my ideal of 
considering ideas with no prejudices, because I'll be able to edit the
territorial instinct out.  The Uploaded will be able to make a conscious
choice, much better than we can, about to what extent their identities
rest in which memes they carry.  Some will probably be much more
conservative about what they edit than others -- but when all it takes
to change one's self is the mere desire to change, I expect conciousness
to quickly differentiate.

This doesn't bug me: I define myself by continuity of experience and very
little else.  I expect to change, and hope to change in very basic radical
ways.  That's what life is about.  My point, though, is that we are often 
recognized as much by our flaws as we are by our virtues -- and if we are
able to toss out what we perceive to be our flaws, we might not be
recognizable.  In other words, we recognize ourselves by very, very
different features than others recognize us by -- they're seperable
concepts.

I'd bet that this will be a debate far more vicious and fractious than
the abortion "when does life begin" argument.  There will be those who
will accept any similar image as concious life and there will be those
who will never accept anything living in an uploaded environment as
life, much less conscious life.

I also strongly suspect that, just like the abortion "when does human
life begin" question, there will never be any strongly agreed upon answer
for what is "conscious" and what is not. "Consciousness" is simply too
poorly defined and internal identity in an entity is too weakly bound to
external identity to other entities.

Musing,
